00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.231
So this is a fairly typical
development team's workflow.

00:01:09.231 --> 00:01:10.730
You can see it's
rendered as a loop.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.009
The idea is that developers
are always making changes.

00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:14.300
They're always pushing changes.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.841
The changes are
constantly integrated

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:19.490
into the main branch of a
shared source code repository.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.084
All of those changes are
subjected to automated tests,

00:01:22.084 --> 00:01:23.750
and ultimately, builds
are automatically

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.330
produced that can be released
into production at will.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:27.830
You want to avoid
the scenario where

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.690
you plan releases over
a period of many months,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.354
and it's a big risky operation.

00:01:32.354 --> 00:01:33.770
The idea is to
automate everything

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.220
from I make a change in
my source code, I push it,

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.110
go through all the
various test suites,

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.420
automatically produce a
build, get it in a state

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.330
that it's ready to be deployed
into production at any time

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:45.449
with the push of a button.

00:01:45.449 --> 00:01:47.240
We don't want this to
be a big risky thing.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.073
It should be boring and
safe, and you should

00:01:49.073 --> 00:01:50.854
be able to do this at any time.

00:01:50.854 --> 00:01:52.520
Keep in mind when we
talk about releases

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.029
throughout this
presentation, we're

00:01:54.029 --> 00:01:55.820
really talking about
any production change.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.015
It can be a code change.

00:01:57.015 --> 00:01:58.640
It can be a change
in the configuration

00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.499
of the application.

00:01:59.499 --> 00:02:01.790
It can even be changes to
the infrastructure underlying

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.010
the application itself.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.010
With this in mind, the
primary goal of Spinnaker

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.180
is to make releases boring,
safe, and repeatable.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.180
Canary releases are just one
tool in the Spinnaker toolbox

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.301
to make releases safer.

00:02:13.301 --> 00:02:15.050
As you hear all of us
talk about canaries,

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.720
and we're going to show all
the different configurations,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.880
and we may here and there
describe it as testing,

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.650
keep in mind it really
is not in any way

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.690
a replacement for
other forms of testing.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.589
As you build out your release
workflow for your shop,

00:02:26.589 --> 00:02:28.880
this is not a replacement
for unit testing, integration

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.700
testing, capacity and load
testing, or any other form

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:32.870
of automated tests.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.550
It's just an additional
thing that you

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.950
can employ with the goal of
increasing your confidence

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.290
that a new release is
safe to fully deploy

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.690
into production without slowing
down your development workflow.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.210
Next up, Chris is
going to come up,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.626
and he's going to
describe exactly

00:02:45.626 --> 00:02:47.120
what a canary release is.

00:03:00.107 --> 00:03:00.940
CHRIS SANDEN: Great.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.080
Thank you, Matt.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.740
So to expand on this idea
of a canary release further,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.170
it is really a deployment
pattern or release

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:09.770
process where a new change--

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:12.574
whether it's code, configuration
as Matt alluded to--

00:03:12.574 --> 00:03:13.990
that it's deployed
into production

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.670
before a subset of our users.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:18.820
And so it gets its
terminology or its name

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.440
from the coal mining
industry where

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.734
miners would take a canary bird
into the coal mine with them.

00:03:23.734 --> 00:03:25.900
And these birds would act
as an early warning signal

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.030
to the buildup of toxic gases.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.370
This would give the coal
miners a sense of safety

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.110
as they worked in the mine.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.540
In a similar way,
a canary release

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.850
can help make a deployment
safer by reducing

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.960
the impact that any one change
can have on your environment.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.760
And this is because
the canary as you

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.100
release it is going to serve a
small subset of your traffic.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.770
If something catastrophic
happens or goes wrong,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.625
it'll only impact a small
percentage of your users.

00:03:52.625 --> 00:03:54.720
And so this can lead
to safer deployments

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.850
by reducing that blast radius.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.890
Now, to emphasize
what Matt has said,

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.530
a canary release is
not a form of testing,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.930
or is not to replace
testing in of itself.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.510
It's just another tool
towards a safer deployment,

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:10.130
and augments or enhances
the CI/CD workflow.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:12.010
Now, as part of
a canary release,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.000
a decision does need
to be made regarding

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.820
the behavior of the canary.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.010
If the behavior of this canary
or a release or a change

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.260
looks good, do we want to
take that and deploy it

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.650
to the rest of production
or to all of our users?

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.810
To help facilitate
this decision making,

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.120
we do need to collect
metrics that describe

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.100
the behavior of the system.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.880
Now, these metrics do need to be
tagged or name spaced in a way

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.500
such that we can easily
retrieve them after the fact.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.260
So you can think of
this as making sure

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.960
that when we report metrics and
report metrics independently

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.420
for the canary and for,
say, our production

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.370
service, so we can
visualize or do

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.500
some analysis later with them.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.360
So once we have a set of metrics
that have been collected,

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.080
a decision is usually
made by looking

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.240
at graphs and dashboards
manually and trying to decide

00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:59.646
does the canary look good?

00:04:59.646 --> 00:05:01.020
And you can kind
of think this is

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.720
a game of spot the
differences, where you're

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.940
looking at the behavior of
the canary as determined

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.610
by the set of metrics in
relation to that production

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:09.289
system.

00:05:09.289 --> 00:05:10.830
And you're really
trying to determine

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.440
if the behavior
of that canary is

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.291
significantly different
than the production system.

00:05:16.291 --> 00:05:17.790
Then based on that
decision, you may

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:21.330
decide to deploy that change or
that canary to all your users

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.730
or to the rest of your
production environment.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:27.760
However, this manual
process of looking

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.760
at graphs and dashboards
can be error prone.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.410
It does require
subjective assessment

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.790
and is prone to human
bias and errors.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:38.560
So I may look at a canary result
or the results of a canary

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.982
and decide it looks good.

00:05:39.982 --> 00:05:42.190
But Matt may come along,
and based on his experience,

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.230
he may look at that
and say, things are OK.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:46.240
Let's push to production.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.670
So in addition, this
process of manually looking

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.680
at graphs and dashboard
is time consuming.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.440
Having someone look at all
the graphs or results just

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.760
doesn't scale.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.655
And as you move towards more
frequent and rapid deployments,

00:05:57.655 --> 00:05:59.530
having someone there to
gate every deployment

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:01.960
is not so feasible.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.360
And then finally, it can
be very difficult to assess

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.100
the quality of the
canary in relation

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.890
to the production's cluster
or the production deployment.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.340
The canary is not serving
the same amount of traffic

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.749
or doing the same type of
workload as production.

00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:16.540
And this makes it very
difficult to compare

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:21.070
the canary to production and get
a good sense of the behavior.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.740
And to address these issues, we
can enhance the canary release

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.660
process in primarily two ways.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:30.340
First, we can introduced an
independent baseline group

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:34.100
or server group as part of
the canary release process.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.680
And so the baseline
here is going

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.890
to be running the same version
or configuration as production.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:40.330
So in our diagram
here, it's going

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.821
to be running, say, version 1.

00:06:42.821 --> 00:06:45.070
At the same time, we're going
to create the new canary

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:46.050
version as well.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.830
And this is going
to be version 2.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.500
Now, the baseline and canary
are going to both be, say,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.930
one server or one
instance, and they're both

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.310
going to serve the exact
same amount of traffic.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.920
So for example, if the canary
is serving 5% of our traffic

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:01.330
and is one server, our baseline
is also going to be one server

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.297
and serving 5% of our traffic.

00:07:03.297 --> 00:07:05.380
Now, its important to
remember that these clusters

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:06.963
or these server
groups are going to be

00:07:06.963 --> 00:07:09.390
serving a very small
percentage of our traffic.

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.320
And again, this is to
limit the blast radius

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:15.850
that any type of change
can have on our end users.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.710
Now, once we have this
environment setup,

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:20.670
we can compare apples to apples.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:23.510
We can now compare the canary
directly to the baseline

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:26.740
and make some type of
automated decision.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:28.810
And this is where
Kayenta comes in.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:31.695
Its a platform for doing
automated canary analysis.

00:07:31.695 --> 00:07:34.320
So Michael will now come up and
talk a little bit about Kayenta

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.295
and how it integrates
with Spinnaker.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.040
MICHAEL GRAFF: Thank you, Chris.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.980
Kayenta is our open
source platform

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:49.720
to allow canary analysis to add
to a pipeline within Spinnaker.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.410
Spinnaker is our open
source deployment platform

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.850
built jointly between
Netflix and Google.

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:57.340
These are all open sourced,
and we have a strong community

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:58.600
around Spinnaker already.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.558
And we're intending to
build a strong community

00:08:00.558 --> 00:08:02.999
around Kayenta, especially
around adding new judges

00:08:02.999 --> 00:08:04.165
and new metric data sources.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:09.960
Spinnaker's role is to
set up the canary such

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.780
that Kayenta can perform
judgments on that data.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:15.370
For example, here
we have a baseline

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.220
which is running exactly
the same production

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:21.000
code or configuration
that the main production

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.350
system is running.

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.282
And the canary is
running the new change.

00:08:24.282 --> 00:08:25.740
It could be a
configuration change,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.010
or it could be a code change.

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:30.900
And Spinnaker will
call upon Kayenta one

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.630
or more times during
some time window

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.549
to perform judgments
on how healthy

00:08:35.549 --> 00:08:38.891
that canary is in
relation to the other.

00:08:38.891 --> 00:08:41.140
How traffic is actually
routed is somewhat application

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:42.610
specific.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.380
One common example
which we'll show later

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.140
is a load balancer,
which is simply however

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:48.820
many instances are
available, each one

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:50.690
gets a certain
percentage of traffic.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:51.820
But in some situations,
you may have

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.361
to choose a more
complicated approach

00:08:53.361 --> 00:08:56.380
where you send 1% or some
small amount of traffic

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:57.991
to your canary and baseline.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.750
This is a simplified
version of a pipeline.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:06.540
In this case, we have
Spinnaker deploying

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:10.340
the canary and the baseline, and
then calling upon Kayenta one

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.280
or more times to get
the individual scores.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.110
In this example, you
have three scores;

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.750
in this case, 90, 92, and 95.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.690
Those could be in different
regions, different locations,

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:22.460
New York and California.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:25.070
They could also be across
multiple time windows.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:27.980
A very common scenario is to
deploy for a four-hour canary

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:30.300
and have 30 minute intervals.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.764
So we wait a small period
to let things warm up,

00:09:32.764 --> 00:09:34.430
and then every 30
minutes after that, we

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.260
perform a canary analysis.

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:38.480
Based upon the
result of that score,

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:39.980
Spinnaker will
decide whether or not

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.290
I should continue
deployment to production

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.925
or roll that back and
cancel the canary operation.

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:51.200
When Kayenta is actually
performing each analysis,

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:53.269
this is the following
steps it takes.

00:09:53.269 --> 00:09:55.310
It will gather the metrics
from both the baseline

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.930
and the canary for each
metric that's defined

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:59.086
in the configuration file.

00:09:59.086 --> 00:10:01.460
That's why it's important that
we can separate those out,

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:02.750
and they have to
be tagged properly

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:04.340
so we know is this
a canary metric,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.802
or is this a baseline
or production metric?

00:10:06.802 --> 00:10:09.010
We'll validate that we got
the data that we expected,

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:11.500
align the data together so
that the similar metrics

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.720
from baselining
and canary lineup.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:15.790
We'll then performs
some cleaning,

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:17.990
make certain the data
is easy to compare,

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:21.359
compare each individual metric
as either a pass, a fail--

00:10:21.359 --> 00:10:22.900
and a fail can be
either high or low.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:24.400
And you can override
which direction

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:28.210
you're sensitive to, so you can
choose high is OK, low is OK.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:30.820
And finally, an individual
score is computed.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:34.010
And this individual score
is a snapshot in time.

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:36.880
This is what the canary
looked like at this moment.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.924
When you do a four-hour
window in multiple canaries,

00:10:39.924 --> 00:10:41.590
you expect each one
to pass, and there's

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:43.215
a lower threshold at
which will cancel,

00:10:43.215 --> 00:10:46.650
and an upper threshold at
the end in which will accept.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:48.630
So here to give a demo is Matt.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.230
MATT DUFTLER: Thanks, Michael.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:56.840
We'll just have to navigate to
the other tabs to do the demo.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.800
We're going to do a live demo.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.650
So fingers crossed.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:02.150
All right.

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:04.310
So just to give you an idea
of what you're going to see,

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.143
we're going to start
with a quick high level

00:11:06.143 --> 00:11:07.370
overview of Spinnaker.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:08.804
We assume some
viewers potentially

00:11:08.804 --> 00:11:10.970
haven't interacted with the
Spinnaker user interface

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:13.170
before, may not be
very familiar with it.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:15.887
So we'll introduce some of
the more important topics.

00:11:15.887 --> 00:11:17.720
And then we'll work
through the full process

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:20.420
from beginning to
end of defining

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:23.810
a canary configuration, figuring
out which metrics to use,

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:26.280
how to apply this to a
set of released pipelines,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.780
we'll walk through
the configuration

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.310
of those pipelines, and then
we'll kick everything off

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:34.670
by pushing some changes which
we'll show in detail, and then

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.836
interact with the results, poke
around the reports and so on.

00:11:37.836 --> 00:11:39.710
So what we're viewing
now is a clusters view.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:42.770
Think of this as the operational
pane or operational view

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:43.599
of Spinnaker.

00:11:43.599 --> 00:11:45.140
It shows all of the
various resources

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:46.490
we have under management.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:47.960
In this case, we're
managing things

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:49.280
deployed through Kubernetes.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.670
So represented on screen now
are four individual deployments

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:55.387
spread across two name spaces
of production and a staging name

00:11:55.387 --> 00:11:56.390
space.

00:11:56.390 --> 00:11:58.040
And this is a sample
application just

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.947
for the purposes of this demo.

00:11:59.947 --> 00:12:02.030
There's a frontend component,
a backend component,

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:04.977
and they constitute a very
simple web application.

00:12:04.977 --> 00:12:06.560
The details of it,
it's written in Go,

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:08.490
but they're not
that significant.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:10.536
But the important
thing to keep in mind

00:12:10.536 --> 00:12:11.910
here, there are
four deployments,

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:13.050
they're already running.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.030
Some in production,
some in staging,

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:17.280
it's a pair of frontend,
backend in each.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.080
So one thing to note
as we look here,

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:21.360
I've highlighted
this deployment.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.372
You can see the details in the
view on the right hand side.

00:12:24.372 --> 00:12:26.580
And you can see each of
these little individual green

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:27.079
chiclets.

00:12:27.079 --> 00:12:28.454
These represent pods.

00:12:28.454 --> 00:12:30.870
And this is the most important
thing to note at the moment

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:32.550
where it says V14.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:35.850
This is associated with the
14th revision of that deployment

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:36.960
within Kubernetes.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:38.550
We have the revision
history set to 0,

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:40.290
so you won't see the older ones.

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:42.484
As they're scaled down to
0, they will be destroyed.

00:12:42.484 --> 00:12:44.400
But the idea is to keep
in mind that revision,

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.570
because as we work through
the release process,

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.611
you'll see that revision
is going to roll forward

00:12:48.611 --> 00:12:50.530
and 15 will show up.

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:53.387
So this is the operational view.

00:12:53.387 --> 00:12:55.470
The pipeline configuration
is the automation view,

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.100
which we're going to
come to in a moment.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:58.200
And remember, our
goal here is we're

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.880
going to configure
a way to experiment

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.710
with pre-configured
pipelines that

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:03.930
use the automated
canary analysis service.

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:06.120
So before we configure the
automated canary analysis

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.040
stage within any
pipelines, we need

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.360
to have some canary
configurations available.

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:13.970
So that's the next view that
we're going to experiment with.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:16.620
So I believe Chris touched
on this or will in a moment.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:19.200
One of the ideas is to
co-locate the configurations

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.940
for the automated
canary analysis

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.280
runs with the
applications themselves.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:24.960
This is tweakable.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.300
You can have it so that
your canary configurations

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.040
can be used across
all applications

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.230
or are scoped to a
set of applications.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:34.274
But the primary goal is that
the operators of these systems

00:13:34.274 --> 00:13:35.940
want to have their
canary configurations

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:38.400
close by to the services
and applications

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:41.830
that they're using those canary
analysis configurations with.

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:44.250
So there's a full user
interface with capabilities

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.790
for editing these things
and managing them over time.

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:49.290
They're all persisted along
with the other artifacts

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:50.460
of Spinnaker.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:52.560
So in this case, we're
deploying to Kubernetes,

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:56.940
and we're using Stackdriver
to monitor those resources.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.120
I would consider
it a prerequisite

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:02.100
of using any of this
that all of your systems

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.450
are properly instrumented.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.516
So if you're not instrumenting
your applications

00:14:05.516 --> 00:14:07.140
and your infrastructure,
there's no way

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.240
you'll be able to select
appropriate metrics later

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.430
on for use in canary analysis.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:14.314
In this case, we selected
three very simple metrics.

00:14:14.314 --> 00:14:15.480
You can see them down below.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.840
We'll just show the
configuration of one of them.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.520
So we're looking
at the CPU request

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:22.470
utilization for containers.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:24.030
Again, it's on Kubernetes.

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:25.330
We give it a name.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.750
We're using this thing we
call a custom filter template.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:30.170
And the idea here
is that you can

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.450
use some of the out of
the box capabilities

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.670
for building the queries,
or you can customize it

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:35.710
any way you like.

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:37.430
It really depends
on your use case.

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:40.320
The most important thing touches
on something that Michael said,

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:43.140
which is that you must know,
in order to use this system,

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:44.300
how to segment your data.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:47.540
If you can't clearly identify
which data was collected

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:50.150
or which metrics were
collected from your baseline

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.329
and which metrics were
collected from your canary,

00:14:52.329 --> 00:14:53.870
you won't be able
to run the analysis

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:57.780
and compare the aggregated
results of each.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:59.520
So we have this
canary configuration.

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:00.217
It has a name.

00:15:00.217 --> 00:15:02.300
And now, we're going to
go over the pipeline view.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:04.758
And we're going to interact
with the pipeline configuration

00:15:04.758 --> 00:15:07.820
and select this canary config
for use as part of a rollout.

00:15:10.491 --> 00:15:10.990
OK.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:13.400
So this is a fairly
typical setup.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:14.600
There are three pipelines.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:16.164
First one is deploy
to staging, then

00:15:16.164 --> 00:15:18.080
we have deploy with
automated canary analysis,

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:20.090
and then promote
canary to production.

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:21.770
The goal of the
first pipeline is

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.350
to be triggered
whenever any push that's

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:26.090
relevant to this
deployment is picked up.

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:29.420
So that can be a change to one
of the underlying containers.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:31.550
That could be a
change to a manifest.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:34.970
In this particular case,
we've configured this pipeline

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:38.480
to kick off whenever frontend
or backend container images are

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:40.760
changed, or when either of
the manifests associated

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:42.860
with those deployments
are changed.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.985
We won't go step-by-step
through the configuration,

00:15:44.985 --> 00:15:46.526
but it's important
to know that's how

00:15:46.526 --> 00:15:48.800
it's kicked off because
we're going to push a change.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.525
And it makes sense to know
where that's coming from.

00:15:51.525 --> 00:15:53.150
So if we look deeper
into the pipeline,

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:54.980
we then see two
parallel deployments.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:56.770
It's going to deploy frontend
and backend, but each of them

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:57.756
are going to staging.

00:15:57.756 --> 00:15:59.630
At this point, we're
not doing any canarying.

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:02.900
The goal here is to show how
we integrate a canary release

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:04.370
into this process.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.650
So we deploy frontend
and backend to staging.

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:08.570
We run some simple
acceptance tests

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:10.430
over the backend,
which in this case,

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:12.650
really amounts to
Kubernetes job,

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:15.704
running a cURL command against
the internal backend service

00:16:15.704 --> 00:16:17.870
in whichever name space
it's in, which in this case,

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:20.910
will be staging.

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:22.470
And lastly, a manual judgment.

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.000
And all that manual judgment
is for is, in this case,

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:27.870
a human to say, I'm happy with
how it worked out in staging.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:31.295
I'm ready to promote
it into production.

00:16:31.295 --> 00:16:33.170
So I'm going to kick
this off, and then we'll

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:35.910
come back and look the
other configurations.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.720
So what we've done here is
make a very simple change

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:41.180
to the manifest that backs
the backend deployment.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:44.930
We've dramatically restricted
the resource allocation for CPU

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:48.365
for the backend so that when
we collect the metrics for CPU

00:16:48.365 --> 00:16:51.080
utilization, it should go
up quite a bit from baseline

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:51.680
to canary.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:54.950
So it has 1/20 of the
allocation of the baseline.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:57.200
So we'll push this change.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:01.030
And all this change is doing,
really, this operation,

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:03.950
is just pushing that updated
manifest to a storage bucket.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:05.530
In this case, it's GCS.

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:09.450
And this pipeline is configured
to listen for pubsub events.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:13.710
So we see it already kicked off
an instance of that workflow.

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:16.175
We would expect the frontend
to complete quite quickly.

00:17:16.175 --> 00:17:18.300
Kubernetes is smart enough
to detect if it's really

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.109
a no op push,
which in this case,

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:21.390
we didn't change the frontend.

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:23.250
It'll complete quicker
than the back end.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:23.970
All right?

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.470
So the backend
will take a moment.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:29.796
It is updating that manifest,
so we should see this--

00:17:29.796 --> 00:17:31.670
obviously, that revision
should roll forward.

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:33.300
And there's the new one, 15.

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:36.510
And then you'll see 14 will
slowly go away over time.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:39.320
If we had left the
revision history set

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:41.550
to anything higher than
0 for that deployment,

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:43.560
you would see the old
revision would remain even

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:44.726
though it would have 0 pods.

00:17:44.726 --> 00:17:47.050
In this case, it will
go away over time.

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:48.930
So we let that carry
on with its work.

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:50.520
We'll go back to
the pipelines view.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:51.440
It's deploying.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:52.530
It'll take a moment.

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:53.550
And what's going
to happen next--

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:55.466
I'll show you the
configuration while we wait.

00:17:55.466 --> 00:17:57.240
Actually, it's going
pretty quickly.

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.080
So [? with ?] the next
pipeline is deployed

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:01.422
with automated canary analysis.

00:18:01.422 --> 00:18:03.630
This pipeline is not configured
to listen for changes

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.830
to the manifests or
the container images.

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:09.100
Its configuration just has
it listening for pipeline

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:10.480
to complete successfully.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:12.350
And it's set to deploy
the staging pipeline.

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:14.350
It will inherit all of
the artifact information.

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.065
So it'll know what
images were deployed,

00:18:16.065 --> 00:18:17.940
it'll will know what
manifests were deployed,

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:20.979
and it can use all that
information in its execution.

00:18:20.979 --> 00:18:22.770
But it's not listening
for changes to them.

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:25.302
It only waits for that
parent pipeline to complete.

00:18:25.302 --> 00:18:26.760
And then what it
does is, again, we

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:28.590
see this parallel deploy scheme.

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:31.130
It's going to, again, deploy
those images and manifests

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.470
that are under test,
frontend and backend.

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:36.080
Only in this case, instead
of deploying the staging,

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:37.680
it's going to deploy
into production,

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.730
then it's going to run
the canary analysis.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:42.450
So let's just OK
this manual judgment,

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:48.100
and then we will carry on with
looking at that configuration.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:49.300
So we'll give that a moment.

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:52.330
While that's thinking, let's
look at our cluster setup.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:54.940
So remember for the next step
that each of the existing

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:56.170
deployments have five pods.

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:58.045
Because as Chris and
Michael both touched on,

00:18:58.045 --> 00:18:59.419
when you do your
canary, you want

00:18:59.419 --> 00:19:01.450
to do it with a smaller
percentage of traffic.

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:04.150
This is a demo, it's
dramatically simplified.

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:05.740
We're not deploying
another baseline.

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:07.198
We're actually
comparing the canary

00:19:07.198 --> 00:19:09.630
to the existing baseline,
which is not ideal.

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:11.380
You want to restrict
the amount of traffic

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:12.490
the canary takes though.

00:19:12.490 --> 00:19:14.694
In this case, where we
have five existing pods,

00:19:14.694 --> 00:19:16.360
we're going to end
up deploying canaries

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:17.870
that have one pod each.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:21.490
It's going to end up taking 1/6
of the traffic, which is a bit,

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.302
but it's a demo.

00:19:23.302 --> 00:19:24.760
So let's look at
the configuration.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:26.370
It's doing the deployments now.

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:29.920
But let's look at this
canary analysis stage.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:31.280
So it's pretty straightforward.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.370
There are not an enormous
amount of configuration options

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:34.160
here at the moment.

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:36.285
But the most important
setting is right at the top.

00:19:36.285 --> 00:19:39.817
And it lets you choose either
real time or retrospective.

00:19:39.817 --> 00:19:42.400
If this were not a demo and we
weren't doing this cooking show

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.002
style, we would do real time.

00:19:44.002 --> 00:19:46.210
The deployments would happen,
and then we would wait.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.020
We would give it
a warm up period.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:50.230
We would wait, let's
say, 20 minutes,

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:54.160
and then maybe do four canaries
over the course of two hours,

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:57.520
and each one would be a
30-minute canary analysis run.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:19:58.801
So that would be real time.

00:19:58.801 --> 00:20:01.300
But we're doing retrospective
because we don't want to wait.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:02.960
Most of the settings
are the same,

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:05.920
except instead of waiting and
then breaking it into pieces,

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:07.450
you encode the time
stamps directly

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:09.247
within the canary
stage configuration.

00:20:09.247 --> 00:20:11.830
So we're using some time stamps
from data we collected earlier

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:12.436
in the day.

00:20:12.436 --> 00:20:13.810
Other than that,
it looks exactly

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.330
as the real time setup would.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.090
So you see here we have location
for baseline and canary.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:21.200
We just give the name
space for each of those.

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:23.750
And then you have
baseline and canary scope.

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:26.440
So the baseline, well,
we just want the primary.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:28.660
As we said, it's just
the baseline deployment

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:30.040
for the backend component.

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:32.854
For canary, we want
backend canary.

00:20:32.854 --> 00:20:34.270
In the case of
this configuration,

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:35.910
this is how we're
segmenting the data.

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:38.160
We're using a naming scheme
and some labels and things

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:38.680
like that.

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:40.030
This depends on your use case.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:41.660
There are lots of
different ways to do this.

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:43.680
Especially with Kubernetes,
the most common case

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.054
is just to assign
labels that let

00:20:45.054 --> 00:20:48.340
you identify where the
metrics were collected from.

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:50.080
So it's really use
case dependent.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.574
This is just one path.

00:20:51.574 --> 00:20:52.990
We say what it is
we're examining.

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.815
They're Kubernetes containers.

00:20:54.815 --> 00:20:56.690
Then we get down to
these scoring thresholds.

00:20:56.690 --> 00:20:58.065
I don't think we
touched on these

00:20:58.065 --> 00:20:59.460
with the canary configuration.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.080
But as you decide
which metrics you

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:04.450
want to query as part of
your canary configuration,

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:06.670
you can group them together
and then assign weights

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:07.529
to those groups.

00:21:07.529 --> 00:21:09.820
And all of that is built up
into a resulting confidence

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:12.100
score for a given
canary analysis run.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:15.460
But as a given run can
contain multiple intervals,

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:18.280
we allow you to specify this
threshold called marginal.

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:20.530
And where this is used is
that as each interval run is

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:22.690
completed, if your
confidence score is

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:25.230
below the marginal threshold,
it just pulls the plug.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:26.612
It says, pull the rip cord.

00:21:26.612 --> 00:21:27.820
We're going to punch out now.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:29.403
We're not going to
do the future runs.

00:21:29.403 --> 00:21:30.610
Don't pay the cost.

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:32.080
So long as all
those interval runs

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:33.820
are above the
marginal threshold,

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:36.910
it will carry on and do
all the future intervals.

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:40.730
Where the pass threshold is used
for the final confidence score.

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:42.760
So the score returned
from the final interval

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:44.410
of the canary analysis runs.

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:46.720
As long as it's above
pass, that canary stage

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:48.370
is considered a success.

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:51.640
If it's below, it's
considered a failure.

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:53.470
As with all Spinnaker
stages, there

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:55.719
are a host of configuration
options about what

00:21:55.719 --> 00:21:57.010
to do in the case of a failure.

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:58.420
Do you halt the whole pipeline?

00:21:58.420 --> 00:21:59.270
Just this branch?

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:00.220
Do you ignore it?

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:00.940
And so on.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:03.080
So it's highly configurable.

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:03.580
All right.

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:05.500
So in this case,
it's hard to read,

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:08.350
I know, but we have a one-hour
duration between start time

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:09.190
and end time.

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:11.270
And we set the
interval to 30 minutes,

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:13.944
so it's going to result in
two canary analysis runs.

00:22:13.944 --> 00:22:15.610
And we set to look
back type to growing.

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:17.026
And what this means
is that you'll

00:22:17.026 --> 00:22:18.496
see a run with 30
minutes of data

00:22:18.496 --> 00:22:20.120
and then a run with
60 minutes of data.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:22.264
You could do sliding
as an alternative.

00:22:22.264 --> 00:22:24.430
As we get into this-- and
we'll talk more about this

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:25.250
in a moment--

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:27.400
but the goal of
retrospective analysis,

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:29.400
while it's sweet
to use for demos,

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:30.910
the real goal is
to allow you later

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:33.040
on to do further
experimentation,

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:34.772
refine your canary
analysis setup,

00:22:34.772 --> 00:22:36.730
and experiment with
different types of judging,

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:39.080
different metrics stores,
different precisions where

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.960
the metrics are
collecting, and so on.

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:44.140
So if we go back to
our view, while it

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:45.680
did the deployment here.

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:47.990
So remember, we said
here, yep, we're happy.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:50.290
We want to deploy the
canary to production.

00:22:50.290 --> 00:22:53.130
It does the deployment
of the front end

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:55.270
to production, the
back end, and then it

00:22:55.270 --> 00:22:58.069
does the canary analysis runs.

00:22:58.069 --> 00:22:59.610
And then you see it
did the two runs.

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:03.170
So you can see duration is 30
minutes, duration is one hour,

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:04.874
it'll show you the
time stamps here.

00:23:04.874 --> 00:23:06.790
And there's this nifty
copy to clipboard thing

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:08.165
if you want to do
a retrospective

00:23:08.165 --> 00:23:10.450
analysis using those values.

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:12.669
But really what we want
to get to is the report.

00:23:12.669 --> 00:23:13.960
So if we click on this report--

00:23:13.960 --> 00:23:15.010
and all of these
UIs are brand new.

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:16.551
It's something we're
really proud of.

00:23:16.551 --> 00:23:18.160
Dan Peach on our
team here at Google

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:20.904
worked with a fellow
named Scott from Netflix

00:23:20.904 --> 00:23:22.570
to build out all these
UIs from scratch,

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:25.090
including the configuration,
the reporting, all of it.

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:25.870
It's really great.

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:27.200
We're pretty pleased with it.

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:29.200
So you can see here, these
are the three metrics

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:31.900
we configured in that canary
config we looked at earlier.

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:32.900
And one of them is high.

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:33.858
The other two are pass.

00:23:33.858 --> 00:23:37.310
We didn't do anything
fancy with the weights.

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:39.550
So you can see just
based on that confidence

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:42.310
score, obviously, it's
2/3 because two passed

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:45.340
and one failed, and they're
equally weighted by default.

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:47.200
But here, you can
see blue is canary,

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:50.077
baseline is this other color,
which I think is copper.

00:23:50.077 --> 00:23:51.410
I'm not sure what color that is.

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:52.570
But you can see
the CP utilization

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:54.550
for that given metric
was obviously much higher

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:58.480
because we really constrained
the resource allocation for CPU

00:23:58.480 --> 00:23:59.960
of that deployment.

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:02.680
So what we've seen here
besides the quick kind

00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:05.020
of 15-second blast
of this is Spinnaker

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:07.150
is what the clusters
look like, which

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:09.670
are deployments because
we're on Kubernetes here,

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:12.100
how to navigate the
operational view of Spinnaker,

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:14.080
how to configure
your pipelines to use

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:16.480
the canary configurations
that you can curate directly

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:18.290
within the user interface.

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:20.980
Then we pushed a change,
which was a modified manifest.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:24.400
We pushed to a GCS
bucket, pops up event,

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:26.220
triggered that first pipeline.

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:27.430
It did the deployments.

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:28.480
It did the simple test.

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:32.190
We said, yep, we're OK, pushed
the canaries into production.

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:33.310
It pushed the canaries.

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:34.501
It ran the analysis.

00:24:34.501 --> 00:24:36.000
If you look at what
was pushed, see,

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.190
it's just pretty cool
in that if you look at--

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:40.398
well, look at the production
environment for backend.

00:24:40.398 --> 00:24:41.320
There are five pods.

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:43.653
And then if you look at backend
canary, there's one pod.

00:24:43.653 --> 00:24:46.810
That's how it's getting that
small amount of traffic.

00:24:46.810 --> 00:24:49.390
And lastly, before I turn
it back over to Chris,

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:51.100
there is another
pipeline here that's

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:53.770
configured to run on
successful completion of deploy

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:55.240
with automated canary analysis.

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:57.310
And this is really the
promotion pipeline.

00:24:57.310 --> 00:24:59.590
If this runs, all it
really does is provision

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:02.380
the new deployments, scale
them up to full capacity,

00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:05.350
and destroy all the Canaries,
and do some cleanup.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:06.250
One last thing.

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:07.750
That canary analysis
stage we showed

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:09.940
is quite simple and
right now focuses

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:12.234
exclusively on configuring
the analysis runs.

00:25:12.234 --> 00:25:13.900
But we're in the
process of building out

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:17.020
full provisioning capabilities
within this stage itself.

00:25:17.020 --> 00:25:18.520
So you can employ
the best practice

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:20.171
that Michael and
Chris described where

00:25:20.171 --> 00:25:21.670
as part of your
canary analysis, you

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:24.640
provision a new
baseline, a new canary,

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.166
and you compare
apples to apples,

00:25:26.166 --> 00:25:27.790
and then do all the
cleanup afterwards,

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:30.260
and there are all sorts of
options associated with that.

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:31.330
So that's in progress.

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:32.663
Now you've seen how to use this.

00:25:32.663 --> 00:25:33.557
Next up is Chris.

00:25:33.557 --> 00:25:35.140
And he's going to
give you some things

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:37.480
to keep in mind,
practical considerations

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:39.957
as you deploy this in
your own environment.

00:26:04.567 --> 00:26:05.400
CHRIS SANDEN: Great.

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:07.170
Thank you very much, Matt.

00:26:07.170 --> 00:26:09.840
So we have been using automated
canary analysis at Netflix

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:12.240
for well over four years
and realizing the benefits

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:14.130
of this style of deployment.

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:15.720
And over these years,
we have learned

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:18.570
many lessons about
canary analysis,

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:22.119
and a few of those we hope
to share with you here today.

00:26:22.119 --> 00:26:23.660
Now, something I do
want to emphasize

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:27.270
is that canary analysis is a
very iterative process that

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:29.670
doesn't need to be refined
over time for, say, each

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:31.500
of your applications.

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:34.270
As your applications
evolve, you make changes,

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:36.337
you add new features, your
canary release process

00:26:36.337 --> 00:26:37.920
and your configuration
that goes along

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:40.639
with it is also going to
change and evolve as well.

00:26:40.639 --> 00:26:42.180
So for example,
let's say that you're

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.160
going to be using
some new hardware

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:46.480
or some new infrastructure
for your application.

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:48.420
This may also change
the metrics or the way

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:50.010
that you perform
a canary, and this

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:53.650
is going to need to be
iterated on as well.

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:56.220
Now, this is particularly
true about the metrics,

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:59.400
which are used to assess or
judge the canary as well.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:07.950
And so we want to select
metrics that describe

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:09.870
the behavior of the system.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:12.810
And this is important for the
success of automated canary

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:13.830
analysis.

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:15.840
Now, the power of
automated canary analysis

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:19.050
comes from, one, the setup
of the infrastructure,

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:21.390
so creating a baseline
of canary groups.

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:23.250
But it also comes
from those metrics

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:24.850
and how we analyze them.

00:27:24.850 --> 00:27:27.900
And so picking those metrics
will take time to get right,

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:30.630
but it is a very important
step to realizing

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:32.670
the benefit of this
approach in gaining trust

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:35.750
in your deployments.

00:27:35.750 --> 00:27:38.360
And so for example, if
your canary is always

00:27:38.360 --> 00:27:41.000
showing differences as
compared to your production

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:43.610
cluster based on some set
of metrics you selected,

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:46.010
you may lose trust in
the system over time.

00:27:46.010 --> 00:27:48.710
And it may lead to more
time for you to investigate

00:27:48.710 --> 00:27:52.460
and will reduce your deployment
cadence and velocity.

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:55.280
Therefore, you will need to
refine these metrics over time

00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:57.560
and to make them very
important to your application

00:27:57.560 --> 00:28:01.510
and to your release
process in general.

00:28:01.510 --> 00:28:03.750
And this is why as part
of our work with Kayenta,

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:05.970
we did integrate the
metric configuration

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:08.040
and the canary
configuration right

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:10.830
into Spinnaker alongside
with your applications.

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:13.770
And Matt had showed a little
bit of this in his demo.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:16.950
Our goal was to make it as easy
as possible to find, update,

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.620
and manage those types of canary
configurations and metrics

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:20.730
definition.

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:22.800
For example, in this
screenshot we see here,

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:25.050
we have a set of metrics
which we are defining,

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:27.540
and they're in a, say, one
group called group 1 here.

00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:31.500
And we want to make these reside
alongside the application.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:34.370
In this case, it's just
an example called My App.

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:37.020
But we can say I have multiple
canary configurations here,

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:38.480
example config and test.

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:39.980
And we want to put
these right along

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:42.520
with the application to
make them easier to find,

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:46.440
but also to allow people to
come in and modify it and update

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:49.710
them along with the rest of
their continuous integration

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:53.500
or continuous
delivery pipelines.

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:55.330
Now, there are a
few best practices

00:28:55.330 --> 00:28:57.640
that you can follow when
trying to select these metrics

00:28:57.640 --> 00:28:59.540
for automated canary analysis.

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:01.360
And so for example,
sparse metrics,

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:04.690
which rarely report
any type of signals,

00:29:04.690 --> 00:29:07.720
are not real good candidates
for canary analysis.

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:09.790
And this is because
of the low reporting

00:29:09.790 --> 00:29:11.020
interval of these metrics.

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:13.510
It would be a little
hard to analyze.

00:29:13.510 --> 00:29:16.810
Now, in contrast to this,
certain types of error metrics

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:19.480
make good candidate signals as
they are very clear indicators

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:21.160
of failure.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:24.280
These metrics can be very easy
to analyze and reason about.

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:26.530
For example, if you're looking
at an increase in error

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:28.960
rates for an HTTP
server, then it

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:30.850
is a very clear
indication of failure

00:29:30.850 --> 00:29:34.960
if your canary has a higher
rate of these types of errors.

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:37.520
And we can see that in the
graph on the right here.

00:29:37.520 --> 00:29:39.280
And finally,
metrics which relate

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:42.130
to application configuration
may not be a good single

00:29:42.130 --> 00:29:43.564
for canary analysis.

00:29:43.564 --> 00:29:44.980
For example, let's
say that you're

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:47.470
looking at the max number
of configured threads

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:48.580
for your applications.

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:51.580
This is not necessarily going to
be a good indicator of failure

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:55.480
as in the face of a failure,
the canary baseline will most

00:29:55.480 --> 00:29:58.122
likely show the same result.
And so these aren't contributing

00:29:58.122 --> 00:29:59.830
anything to your canary
analysis and will

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:04.824
want to be removed or
tweaked for your analysis.

00:30:04.824 --> 00:30:06.990
Now, in addition to picking
the right metrics, which

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:08.820
is very important,
there are also

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:11.190
some other practical
considerations to keep in mind

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:14.530
or think about as you
employ canary analysis.

00:30:14.530 --> 00:30:17.420
For example, when an
application does start up,

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:20.920
it may throw more errors
or have higher utilization,

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:24.177
and this can be because of
caches failing, getting warm.

00:30:24.177 --> 00:30:26.010
It can also be because
of garbage collection

00:30:26.010 --> 00:30:28.176
or the application establishing
connections to, say,

00:30:28.176 --> 00:30:29.802
downstreams or upstreams.

00:30:29.802 --> 00:30:31.260
Now, these types
of startup effects

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:33.800
will skew the results
of canary analysis.

00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:35.550
And for this reason,
you'll want to ignore

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:40.460
these types of startup effects
as part of your canary release.

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:42.750
And so within Spinnaker
and within Kayenta,

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:45.000
we allow you to configure
a warm-up period.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.950
And for example, we can see
that we're, in this example,

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:50.045
setting a warm-up
period of 10 minutes.

00:30:50.045 --> 00:30:52.170
In this example, we're
going to ignore the first 10

00:30:52.170 --> 00:30:55.107
minutes of the metrics and
then perform the analysis.

00:30:55.107 --> 00:30:57.690
This will give you a little bit
better confidence in your data

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:01.540
and into canary release process.

00:31:01.540 --> 00:31:03.970
And finally, you'll
want to consider

00:31:03.970 --> 00:31:06.070
how big to make your
canary deployment

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:09.140
or how much traffic your canary
release is going to take.

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:11.980
So in this example we have
here, our canary deployment

00:31:11.980 --> 00:31:14.590
is going to be taking
25% of our traffic.

00:31:14.590 --> 00:31:17.020
This example is the
traffic is routed

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:18.970
based on a load balancer.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:21.280
So this means that
25% of our traffic

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:24.916
is at risk of this new change
or this canary we are releasing.

00:31:24.916 --> 00:31:26.290
Now, this is
contrary to the idea

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:28.660
that a canary is
about reducing risk.

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:30.880
And so there are some
balances or trade offs

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:33.550
you'll have to make in terms
of how big to make the canary

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:35.496
or how small the
make the canary.

00:31:35.496 --> 00:31:37.870
And so in this example here,
we may want to use a smaller

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:39.970
number of servers-- maybe
we use only one server

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:40.870
for the canary--

00:31:40.870 --> 00:31:42.670
or find a different
way for our traffic

00:31:42.670 --> 00:31:44.740
to make its way to the canary.

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:48.980
You can also debate, should we
even use a canary in this case?

00:31:48.980 --> 00:31:52.170
Let's say that my application
is one instance or one server.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:54.430
A canary may not
be the right tool

00:31:54.430 --> 00:31:58.442
to use in this case to
mitigate that risk of a change.

00:31:58.442 --> 00:32:00.400
So up next, Steven's
going to talk a little bit

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:03.040
how you can get started
with Spinnaker and Kayenta.

00:32:08.245 --> 00:32:08.870
STEVEN KIM: Hi.

00:32:08.870 --> 00:32:11.260
I'll point you in a couple
of directions by which you

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:16.230
can get started on your journey
to doing canary analysis based

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:21.020
rollouts on your project
or in your organizations.

00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:25.460
First, you need to
install Spinnaker.

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:28.370
Down below on the screen,
there's spinnaker.io,

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:30.260
our website, /setup.

00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:31.970
There are a number
of ways that you

00:32:31.970 --> 00:32:35.030
can go to install Spinnaker
on various different platforms

00:32:35.030 --> 00:32:36.791
in different configurations.

00:32:36.791 --> 00:32:38.540
And there, you can get
all the information

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:41.012
you need to get
Spinnaker installed.

00:32:41.012 --> 00:32:43.220
For those of you who have
already installed Spinnaker

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:45.260
and are running
Spinnaker, obviously, this

00:32:45.260 --> 00:32:47.060
is a no op for you.

00:32:47.060 --> 00:32:49.400
Second, you need
to enable Kayenta.

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:53.300
And once again, on
spinnaker.io/setup/canary,

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:56.180
you'll find detailed
instructions for setup

00:32:56.180 --> 00:33:00.770
and guides for how you can get
Kayenta canary analysis set up

00:33:00.770 --> 00:33:02.450
on your Spinnaker instance.

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:07.680
And third, aside from directly
Spinnaker or Kayenta related,

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:09.680
you need to ensure
that your applications

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:11.270
and your infrastructure
metrics are

00:33:11.270 --> 00:33:13.940
being reported to
your metric store

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:17.060
so that Kayenta can
then act on those.

00:33:17.060 --> 00:33:20.030
So after the base setups
are done on the top row,

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:23.390
on the bottom row is about
you starting on your journey

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:27.020
that Chris mentioned
of figuring out

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:30.680
what are the right metrics
for your particular situation

00:33:30.680 --> 00:33:34.640
and that iterative refining and
ongoing process of improving

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:35.750
your canary signals.

00:33:35.750 --> 00:33:38.150
So number one,
identifying the metrics

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:39.710
that are strong
indicators of safety

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:43.130
for your particular
application and infrastructure

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:46.940
among the set that you set
up on the previous step

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:50.660
of infrastructure application
metrics being collected.

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:53.012
And then finally,
configure your canaries.

00:33:53.012 --> 00:33:54.470
And once again, as
Chris mentioned,

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:58.000
this is going to be an
iterative and continued process

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.740
for you to do so.

00:34:00.740 --> 00:34:03.290
We're very proud of the
collaborative good work

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:04.440
that we've done here.

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:06.960
Thanks for joining us.

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:10.040
We want to invite
you to continue

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:14.469
to join us by joining in
on the community on Slack,

00:34:14.469 --> 00:34:17.150
on join.spinnaker.io where
you can go to reach us

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:19.638
with any questions or for help.

00:34:19.638 --> 00:34:21.179
There's plenty of
documentation, as I

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:24.590
mentioned, on our main
website, spinnaker.io.

00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:27.110
And then, of course, we
also welcome contributors,

00:34:27.110 --> 00:34:30.530
whether they are bug
fixes, small features,

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:35.150
or large things such as
adding an entire metric store.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:39.699
Our project is open source, and
you can find our code on GitHub

00:34:39.699 --> 00:34:43.280
at /spinnaker/kayenta.

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:46.520
So stay tuned for live Q&amp;A next.

00:34:46.520 --> 00:34:47.929
We'll be back in under a minute.

00:35:47.025 --> 00:35:48.150
MATT DUFTLER: Welcome back.

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:48.390
All right.

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:50.056
We're going to do
some Q&amp;A. So these are

00:35:50.056 --> 00:35:51.400
questions from the audience.

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:53.175
And I'll lead off with
the first question.

00:35:53.175 --> 00:35:55.800
"Is it possible to override the
results of the automated canary

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:56.987
analysis?

00:35:56.987 --> 00:35:59.070
What if I need to deploy
even if the system thinks

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:00.210
the canary failed?"

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:00.710
Yeah.

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:02.530
So there are a couple
of answers to this.

00:36:02.530 --> 00:36:02.760
Yep.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:04.650
So it's pretty
straightforward to override

00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:08.970
the result. Spinnaker itself,
nothing specific to Kayenta,

00:36:08.970 --> 00:36:11.670
supports the ability to
have both conditional stages

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:14.010
and branch operations, and
you can use expressions

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:14.920
in those conditions.

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:17.160
So you can very
easily do one thing

00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:20.010
if the analysis passed,
one if it failed,

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:22.290
and join those back up
in a manual judgment.

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:25.380
So that's fairly
straightforward to configure.

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:27.840
More importantly,
if you're noticing

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:30.740
that folks are overriding the
decision of the canary analysis

00:36:30.740 --> 00:36:35.010
often, you might want to revisit
that actual configuration,

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:37.290
see if it needs to be
refined because it probably

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:40.710
is a signal that folks lack
trust in the canary analysis,

00:36:40.710 --> 00:36:43.620
maybe it's the wrong metrics,
maybe it runs for too

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:45.204
short of a period of time.

00:36:45.204 --> 00:36:46.620
There could be
some reason why you

00:36:46.620 --> 00:36:49.080
need to actually
address the root cause

00:36:49.080 --> 00:36:53.100
and not just override
the decision and move on.

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:55.280
All right.

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:57.357
I think we can both
do the next one.

00:36:57.357 --> 00:36:58.440
So I'll read the question.

00:36:58.440 --> 00:36:59.820
"What's next for Kayenta?

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:03.180
Could you give some details on
how the automatic optimization

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:06.135
process could work and why
we would want to do that?"

00:37:06.135 --> 00:37:07.740
You want to lead off and then--

00:37:07.740 --> 00:37:08.010
CHRIS SANDEN: Sure.

00:37:08.010 --> 00:37:08.330
So--

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:09.663
MATT DUFTLER: --comment as well.

00:37:09.663 --> 00:37:11.380
CHRIS SANDEN: So what's
next for Kayenta?

00:37:11.380 --> 00:37:13.130
There's a few avenues
that we're exploring

00:37:13.130 --> 00:37:14.921
for the future of
Kayenta that relates more

00:37:14.921 --> 00:37:16.650
to the analysis side of things.

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:18.300
I'll let Matt talk
a little bit more

00:37:18.300 --> 00:37:22.250
of the infrastructure and the
Spinnaker work after myself.

00:37:22.250 --> 00:37:24.750
And so some of the work we're
doing for Kayenta and where

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:26.790
we want to expand
and move towards

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:30.150
is making our results a
little more practical.

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:33.630
So right now we see a lot of
sensitivity in our approach.

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:35.400
And some of the
metric classifications

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:38.040
or metric results we give are
not necessarily as practical

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:39.810
as our end users would like.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:42.360
So we want to spend some
time to revisit, how do we

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:45.060
make our results a little
more practical for end users

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:46.050
to consume?

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:47.414
The idea is to reduce risk.

00:37:47.414 --> 00:37:49.830
We also want to make these
results practical and something

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:53.250
that you can use to gain
safety in your deployments.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:55.970
In addition, as described
through the demo

00:37:55.970 --> 00:37:58.050
and throughout the
presentation, Kayenta

00:37:58.050 --> 00:38:00.390
is very much a mini
batch or micro batch

00:38:00.390 --> 00:38:02.400
system where over
some period of time,

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:06.030
we're performing little
batches of analysis over time.

00:38:06.030 --> 00:38:07.680
We'd like to
investigate or move more

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:10.770
towards a continuous
evaluation approach where we're

00:38:10.770 --> 00:38:12.990
continually evaluating over
an entire period of time,

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:15.780
say, every minute
generating a score.

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:18.654
This allows us potentially
to fail even faster.

00:38:18.654 --> 00:38:20.070
So we look at a
canary deployment,

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:22.220
and we look at the
risk is the greatest

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:24.510
in the first hour, in
the first 30 minutes,

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:26.470
or the first 15 minutes.

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:29.160
And so finding those failures
or those issues faster

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:32.990
is very critical to this
system managing that risk.

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:35.670
And so we want to work at
failing faster and finding

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:40.610
those big differences and those
big, impactful changes sooner.

00:38:40.610 --> 00:38:42.110
And this will help
mitigate the risk

00:38:42.110 --> 00:38:45.057
to be able to roll your
deployments back faster.

00:38:45.057 --> 00:38:45.890
MATT DUFTLER: Great.

00:38:45.890 --> 00:38:46.830
Thanks.

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:47.370
OK.

00:38:47.370 --> 00:38:48.870
In terms of what's
next for Kayenta,

00:38:48.870 --> 00:38:52.020
I can give some information
on what's next for the project

00:38:52.020 --> 00:38:52.800
itself.

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:54.405
We've made all this available.

00:38:54.405 --> 00:38:55.380
It works quite well.

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:57.810
We're very happy with the
current state of things.

00:38:57.810 --> 00:38:59.940
Now we'd really like
to focus on adoption,

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:02.310
making it easy for folks
to get up and running.

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:04.090
We have quite a bit
of documentation,

00:39:04.090 --> 00:39:05.580
but we'd like more, obviously.

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:08.490
We want to build out code
labs, potentially a workshop.

00:39:08.490 --> 00:39:11.020
And most importantly, we
want to grow the community.

00:39:11.020 --> 00:39:13.860
We have support at the moment
for Stackdriver, Atlas,

00:39:13.860 --> 00:39:15.630
Prometheus, and Datadog.

00:39:15.630 --> 00:39:18.396
The Datadog support
was added by Armory.

00:39:18.396 --> 00:39:19.770
We'd like to repeat
that process,

00:39:19.770 --> 00:39:23.370
have other active participants
in the Spinnaker community

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:25.890
add support for
additional metric stores.

00:39:25.890 --> 00:39:28.710
There's a pretty well-defined
path for adding support

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:30.790
for additional metric stores.

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:34.380
We have a development guide,
and we have very easy to follow

00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:36.390
commits that
explain step-by-step

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:37.950
how to add support
for new systems,

00:39:37.950 --> 00:39:39.390
or add new integrations.

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:42.300
And in addition, we built
Kayenta from scratch

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:44.040
to be easy to develop within.

00:39:44.040 --> 00:39:45.630
So everything is exposed.

00:39:45.630 --> 00:39:47.250
There are troubleshooting
endpoints.

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:48.430
Everything is swaggered.

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:49.770
It's pretty well documented.

00:39:49.770 --> 00:39:51.840
So it's a pretty
straightforward process.

00:39:51.840 --> 00:39:53.920
It's not trivial, but
it's definitely doable,

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:55.284
and it's quite straightforward.

00:39:55.284 --> 00:39:56.700
CHRIS SANDEN: I
think what also is

00:39:56.700 --> 00:39:59.852
next is more engagement
from the community

00:39:59.852 --> 00:40:01.560
and getting everyone
else involved in it,

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:04.710
contributing, and also expanding
our documentation and our best

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:06.090
practices.

00:40:06.090 --> 00:40:08.017
We want to be able to
bundle in best practices

00:40:08.017 --> 00:40:09.600
with Kayenta, so
whether that includes

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:13.230
example configurations of
metrics or example pipelines.

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:15.269
Now, that's also in
terms of getting feedback

00:40:15.269 --> 00:40:17.310
from you, the people out
there wanting to use it,

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:18.649
and iterating on that as well.

00:40:18.649 --> 00:40:19.440
MATT DUFTLER: Yeah.

00:40:19.440 --> 00:40:22.470
We had talked a couple of times
about maybe making it smarter

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:24.210
in terms of suggesting
optimizations

00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:26.940
to canary configurations,
or even proposing

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:28.290
canary configurations.

00:40:28.290 --> 00:40:30.480
When there was an issue
that a human responded to,

00:40:30.480 --> 00:40:32.880
maybe human can come back
and say, for this release,

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:34.440
for this scope--

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:36.790
so these time boundaries
with these resources--

00:40:36.790 --> 00:40:39.160
what sort of canary process
would have caught this,

00:40:39.160 --> 00:40:41.410
would have sussed out that
there's a problem and said,

00:40:41.410 --> 00:40:42.910
this is not a safe deployment?

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:45.035
So we think we can get
smarter about that over time

00:40:45.035 --> 00:40:46.980
and really not leave
folks with blank screens

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:49.146
where they have to figure
out all this configuration

00:40:49.146 --> 00:40:50.290
from scratch.

00:40:50.290 --> 00:40:50.860
All right.

00:40:50.860 --> 00:40:52.485
So the next question
is, "documentation

00:40:52.485 --> 00:40:55.140
mentions real time and
retrospective evaluation.

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:57.876
Can you give some pointers
regarding which to use when?"

00:40:57.876 --> 00:40:59.750
All right, I'll lead
off, and you can jump in

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:01.360
if you want to add something.

00:41:01.360 --> 00:41:03.540
So real time, we
touched on it a bit.

00:41:03.540 --> 00:41:06.850
The idea is as part
of a release pipeline,

00:41:06.850 --> 00:41:08.809
you provision the
resources, and then

00:41:08.809 --> 00:41:10.350
you want to compare
the metrics being

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:13.020
collected from those resources
you just provisioned.

00:41:13.020 --> 00:41:15.180
So you would have
typically a warm-up period.

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:17.640
And then for both the
newly provisioned baseline

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:20.300
and production and
canary deployments,

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:22.800
you would compare those metrics
to one another in real time,

00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:25.510
meaning the metrics collected
over the last hour, two hours,

00:41:25.510 --> 00:41:26.400
three hours.

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:29.640
Retrospective is similar
for all of the configuration

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:31.560
with the exception of
the timing information.

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:34.080
Instead of saying, start now,
wait this period of time,

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:36.150
and then do these
intervals, it says,

00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:39.450
for these given encoded
timestamps, do the analysis.

00:41:39.450 --> 00:41:41.220
You can still do
it in intervals,

00:41:41.220 --> 00:41:44.830
but it refers to
past points in time.

00:41:44.830 --> 00:41:46.050
The benefit of this--

00:41:46.050 --> 00:41:48.276
besides being much
easier to demo--

00:41:48.276 --> 00:41:50.650
is that you can experiment
with different configurations.

00:41:50.650 --> 00:41:53.250
So different judges,
different metric stores,

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:56.610
more precise, less precise data,
more metrics, less metrics,

00:41:56.610 --> 00:41:58.600
and so on.

00:41:58.600 --> 00:41:59.100
All right.

00:41:59.100 --> 00:42:00.684
I think the next
one's probably yours.

00:42:00.684 --> 00:42:02.516
CHRIS SANDEN: So, "can
you talk a little bit

00:42:02.516 --> 00:42:04.740
about your experience with
optimizing canary analysis

00:42:04.740 --> 00:42:06.060
for applications?

00:42:06.060 --> 00:42:08.600
What would your top three
lessons, bits of advice be?"

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:10.170
Its a good question.

00:42:10.170 --> 00:42:12.450
Much like the practical
considerations

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:14.490
is I think one of the
big lessons to take away

00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:15.990
is that the metrics matter.

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:18.780
It is the signals you
put into the system

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:20.700
are going to be very
important to the success

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:21.680
of canary analysis.

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:23.440
Kind of idea of garbage
in, garbage out.

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:26.339
You give it bad metrics, you
potentially get bad results.

00:42:26.339 --> 00:42:28.380
And so there is this time,
this iterative process

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:31.344
of taking your canary release
and iterating on those metrics

00:42:31.344 --> 00:42:32.760
and finding the
right signals that

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:36.690
are going to be successful
for doing a canary analysis.

00:42:36.690 --> 00:42:38.340
And kind of piggybacking
off that idea

00:42:38.340 --> 00:42:40.500
of putting the right
metrics into the system

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:42.690
is making sure that your
infrastructure is set up

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:43.524
correctly.

00:42:43.524 --> 00:42:44.940
And well, for
example, making sure

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:46.560
that that baseline
and that canary

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:50.250
are both running in the
production in the same region

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:51.970
taking the same
amount of traffic.

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:54.180
The experimental setup or
the infrastructure setup

00:42:54.180 --> 00:42:57.934
is very crucial to making
canary analysis a success.

00:42:57.934 --> 00:42:59.850
And so having that
combination of a good setup

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.550
and a good experiment set
up, the right metrics, really

00:43:02.550 --> 00:43:06.360
alleviates a lot of the issues
with downstream canary analysis

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:09.190
with why did this fail or
why did I see a difference?

00:43:09.190 --> 00:43:11.340
So focusing on kind of
the setup and the metrics

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:13.815
can alleviate a lot of those
problems down the road.

00:43:13.815 --> 00:43:14.220
MATT DUFTLER: Yeah.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:15.170
This is a really great point.

00:43:15.170 --> 00:43:17.220
If you can't [? absent ?]
Spinnaker and Kayenta,

00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:19.220
clearly identify
how to segment--

00:43:19.220 --> 00:43:21.210
along what dimension
to segment the metrics

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:22.710
that you've collected--
you won't be

00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:23.850
able to perform the analysis.

00:43:23.850 --> 00:43:25.516
Kayenta isn't going
to magically discern

00:43:25.516 --> 00:43:27.690
the difference between
metrics you've collected.

00:43:27.690 --> 00:43:29.190
You have to be able
to collect them,

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:30.606
identify the two
groups of metrics

00:43:30.606 --> 00:43:32.610
that you've collected
that you want to compare.

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:34.140
That's what the
judge considers when

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:35.760
it's performing its analysis.

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:37.600
So that's a great point.

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:40.431
CHRIS SANDEN: So with that,
I think we're out of time.

00:43:40.431 --> 00:43:42.180
MATT DUFTLER: Continue
to look for answers

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:43.096
your questions, folks.

00:43:43.096 --> 00:43:46.170
We'll be answering those in the
setup after the webinar ends.

00:43:46.170 --> 00:43:47.560
Thanks, everybody,
for your time.

00:43:47.560 --> 00:43:49.643
Stay tuned for the next
session "Build Modern Apps

00:43:49.643 --> 00:43:52.380
Using an API First Strategy."

